WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:03.910
(gentle uplifting music)

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:05.320
- So the past year and a half,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.520
I've really been made aware
of the intimate relationship

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.880
between our psychology and our physiology.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.410
It seemed like as we were
locked down physically,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.610
we were also locked down mentally.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.570
As we felt kind of stuck in our own homes

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.260
and in our apartments,

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.320
we also felt stuck inside our own heads.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.630
We fell into cyclical thinking,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.360
and I think a lot of that has to do

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.380
with the fact that there
weren't fresh inputs,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.070
no fresh experiences
being added into our lives

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.260
so we didn't have novel ideas.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.930
It was all exacerbated as well by the fact

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.500
that the algorithms that we
were likely leaning on heavily

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.730
through YouTube and Netflix and Twitter

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.480
and all of that learn
what we tend to click on.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.500
So we start to develop
these deep digital grooves

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.700
into certain topics of interest,

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.970
and you start getting recommended

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.050
the same content day in and day out

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.200
and you start to wonder,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.760
like, why am I stuck in a rut?

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.420
Why am I thinking the same things

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.010
that I'm thinking all the time

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.310
and how come I can't
get out of this pattern?

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.830
When you take a step back from it all,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.490
it all becomes pretty obvious,

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.730
but when you're trapped in it,

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.600
it's hard to gain the
perspective that you need

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.540
to get out of that situation
and into a better one.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.610
And that's another thing
that's really become apparent

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.750
about human nature over the past year

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.970
is that when we don't have
that exterior influence,

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.110
when we don't talk to new people,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.050
when we don't have a sort
of an outside network

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.390
and we're just trapped
inside our own heads,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.870
it's almost always a really bad thing

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.450
over a long period of time.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.320
This isolation almost
always breeds a pessimism,

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.170
negative thought patterns
while it might be good

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.600
to be alone sometimes and learn
to be alone with yourself,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.440
too much of that, too much isolation,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.810
almost always corrupts us.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.860
It seems like human
beings operate optimally

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.440
when we're working together,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.320
when we're giving each other new ideas,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.890
when we're helping each other out,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.000
when we're carrying a load
and accepting responsibility

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.670
to help the group and it's this
putting yourself out there,

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.710
this voluntary acceptance
of responsibility

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.130
that kind of gives life its meaning.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.840
The existential dread of
existence can be kept at bay

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.490
when it feels like you're
contributing to something.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.980
As we play a role in
these sub-communities,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.470
in these sports teams,
in these book clubs,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.850
in these volunteer opportunities,

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:31.000
our life seems bigger and more optimistic,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.110
full of more opportunity.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.720
We're not so trapped inside

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.620
our own pessimistic way of thinking.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.450
I feel like this is all because
there's an intimate link

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.530
between our psychology
and the physical space

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:43.770
that we take up.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.600
The places we go and the
people we interact with

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.880
are extensions of our consciousness.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.420
And through these external influences,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.920
we gain access to new
ideas, new ways of thinking.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.610
And we unlock parts of the brain

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.510
that we would not have
access to in solitude.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:03.210
There's a reason why so many
self-improvement junkies

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.530
or gurus or psychologist,

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.250
Navy Seals will tell you to
make your bed, clean your room,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.110
organize the physical space around you.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.760
Jocko Willink, Jordan
Peterson, you name it,

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.980
these people are all saying
that the physical environment

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.220
in which you are a part of
is an extension of yourself.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.980
And following the same train of thought,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.790
I would also say the physical size,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:31.460
the literal square footage
of your physical surroundings

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.260
is sort of the square footage
of your mental surroundings,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.340
of your existential surroundings.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.060
And that feels weird to say,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.910
but I feel like people kind
of know this instinctually,

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.230
if you never ever leave your house

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.360
and you're just trapped
living the same day over

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.970
and over and over again,

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.780
then your existence really
starts to feel small.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.390
The literal amount of
things that you influence

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.860
on a day-to-day basis is quite small.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.560
Your life can start to
feel very insignificant.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.420
You don't actually make a difference

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.130
in the world as much as you would

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:05.570
if you were out there
interacting with more things.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.500
And it's the same reason
why people who travel a lot,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.890
they get back from a year of traveling.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.660
They have so many new
ideas and perspectives

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.990
and they're so inspired and full of life.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.190
And that's because by physically
traveling to new places,

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.440
they mentally travel
to new places as well.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.340
Their existence seems larger.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.670
And if you don't have the luxury

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.070
to travel around everywhere at your whims,

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.770
and I know a lot of
people absolutely don't,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.570
this still applies to whatever
town or city you live in.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:38.070
It applies to the activities
that you say yes or no to.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.340
By actually saying yes to
that salsa dancing class

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.570
or that Brazilian jujitsu class

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.150
or saying yes to playing
volleyball on the beach

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.930
with your brother and his
wife, these things matter.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.520
New experiences open your world up

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.030
and make your life feel more significant.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.010
This was all just
basically a long-winded way

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.370
of saying, get outside.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.370
Obviously not in a way that
will hurt or endanger anybody,

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.040
but you guys are smart
by injecting yourself

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.090
into the world around you
instead of hiding away

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.920
in a tower like Rapunzel,

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.850
you'll actually start
to feel like you have

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.950
some autonomy over your own life.

